A||||7||Alex2004||Blue smoke at first start up....why?||||3||
Z||000000||Alex2004||12-13-2001||09:25 PM||||I have an 84 SE with 97K on it and about a year ago i just started noticing blue smoke at start up, now it's getting alittle worse over time.  My car isn't burning oil though.  But what causes this, is there an additive i can use to get rid of it, and what needs to be done to get rid of it?  Thanks for any help!<P>Alex...||||reg||3
Z||000001||Fiero_24||12-13-2001||09:49 PM||||Old valve seals.  Replace them and the problem will go away.  You might want to try some of the new oils for higher milage engines, the supposedly help with this problem.  I have never tried them.<P>------------------<BR>    <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/driverside41.jpg"> <BR>  <A HREF="http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24" TARGET=_blank>http://www.geocities.com/fiero_24</A> <BR>1987 GT||||reg||7
Z||000002||CanadianGT||12-13-2001||09:50 PM||||That is the sign of leaking valve guides/seals<BR>PeAcE <BR>K Mo||||reg||1
Z||000003||ToddAsh||12-13-2001||09:53 PM|||| If the weathers not to cold 30W oil (not 10w30) will really cut the smoking down.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Smoooooth GT||12-14-2001||03:49 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fiero_24:<BR><B>Old valve seals.  Replace them and the problem will go away.  <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fiero 24,<BR>    Hi, I have a question..  How hard of a job is it to change the seal's??? How long should it take to do the job on average??  Thank's for the input...  Steve<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/vroom_5.gif">        <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/smooooothgtsig_9.gif">                <BR>      <P> <A HREF="http://www.thefibersmith.com" TARGET=_blank>www.thefibersmith.com</A> <P>Technical  Support (903) 825-7744 Steve<BR>Shaved door handles, sidetrim, exhaust outlet's, etc.... smooooth!!!||||reg||1
Z||000005||deceler8||12-14-2001||12:34 PM||||I'm not sure the "older engine" oil will help with this...I can't imagine how an additive will repair cracked or missing rubber valve seals.<P>Or will it ? It might "swell" some kinds of seals and slow leakage, like through an old style rear main seal, but I can't imagine it works on O'rings.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ToddAsh||12-16-2001||02:43 PM||||It's not the additives.30W oil is Way thicker than 10W30 which is really 10W oil that protects like 30W when fully heated up. 30W takes much longer to leak past the seals,rings,etc.It cracks me up when people switch from 10W30 to 10W40 and they think theyr'e using a heavier oil.Been racing cars for 30 years and we run straight 40W in our big block chevies.10W40 would give no oil pressure and leak past everything.||||reg||1
Z||000007||rogergarrison||12-16-2001||03:45 PM||||I wouldnt bother with it unless your using lots of oil between changes. Mines done it since brand new rebuild and has done it for 90K miles. I average 5,000 miles between changes and never add any. Its just one of those things at least half of all Fiero V6s do. It is the valve seals. But really not worth messing with.<P>------------------<BR>           <IMG SRC="http://www.jadeweb.com.au/fiero/rogergarrison1.gif"> ||||reg||1
